# Structure

Put the thing a household needs most at the top of the screen, then routine readings, then controls they use less often. Start with a phone layout and let Home Assistant add columns as the screen gets wider.

A predictable order helps people who check the same dashboard many times a day. They learn where the front door lock is and find it without reading every card.

## Structure pages

- [Page template](structure-page-template.html): the parts of a dashboard view, in order, inside the Home Assistant frame.
- [Screen size](structure-screen-size.html): the 4 Home Assistant screen sizes and one dashboard on a phone, a tablet and a wall panel.
- [Common layouts](structure-common-layouts.html): one column, a wide section beside narrow ones, and one card across the whole view.
- [Setting up page wrappers](structure-page-wrappers.html): what the view, the section and the card each control.
- [Using the grid system](structure-grid.html): the 12-column section grid and card widths.
- [Width overrides](structure-width-overrides.html): how to make a section or a card wider or narrower.
- [Override how elements display](structure-display-overrides.html): visibility conditions and the show options on a card.
- [Hide elements and keep them accessible to screen readers](structure-hiding-elements.html): text that only screen readers announce, in a maintained custom card.
- [Spacing](structure-spacing.html): the Civica 5px spacing scale and the native Home Assistant spacing.
- [Section break](structure-section-break.html): how to separate groups of cards, and when to add a border.
